WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.511
Some questions you cannot
answer out of your own head.

2
00:00:07.731 --> 00:00:10.843
You have to ask, count,
and chart what people say.

3
00:00:11.343 --> 00:00:14.919
Some questions you can just
answer. Some need data.

4
00:00:15.139 --> 00:00:19.458
How many legs has a spider?
Eight, and everybody says eight.

5
00:00:19.678 --> 00:00:23.393
Nobody knows how we travel to
school until you ask us all.

6
00:00:23.613 --> 00:00:26.353
Those answers vary - worth asking.

7
00:00:26.853 --> 00:00:29.871
A tally counts things while
they are still happening.

8
00:00:30.091 --> 00:00:33.946
One mark for each child who
walks. One, two, three, four.

9
00:00:34.166 --> 00:00:37.881
The fifth mark lies across
the other four, like a gate.

10
00:00:38.101 --> 00:00:41.538
Now count the bundles in
fives, then the ones left over.

11
00:00:41.758 --> 00:00:45.194
One bundle is five, and four
more makes nine children.

12
00:00:45.414 --> 00:00:49.129
Write it in a table too.
That is a frequency table.

13
00:00:49.629 --> 00:00:56.317
A pictogram shows counts as pictures. Read
the key: one picture means two children.

14
00:00:56.537 --> 00:01:00.438
Apples has five pictures.
Five twos make ten children.

15
00:01:00.658 --> 00:01:04.605
Counting the pictures gives five,
and five is the wrong answer.

16
00:01:04.825 --> 00:01:08.912
Oranges has one picture and a
half: two and one make three.

17
00:01:09.412 --> 00:01:13.731
The same counts, drawn as bars.
Taller bar, more children.

18
00:01:13.951 --> 00:01:17.527
Along the bottom is each
way. Up the side is how many.

19
00:01:17.747 --> 00:01:21.833
Walk is the tallest bar, at
nine. Car is shortest, at three.

20
00:01:22.053 --> 00:01:30.041
Nine take away three is six. Altogether, twenty-two
children answered, six more by walking than by car.

21
00:01:30.541 --> 00:01:33.420
Sometimes you sort things
instead of counting them.

22
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.913
Two hoops: even numbers in one,
more than twenty in the other.

23
00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:42.452
Eight is even, but not more than
twenty. It goes in the even hoop.

24
00:01:42.672 --> 00:01:46.573
Thirty-three is more than twenty,
but it is odd. The other hoop.

25
00:01:46.793 --> 00:01:50.879
Twenty-four is both, so it goes
in the middle, where they overlap.

26
00:01:51.099 --> 00:01:55.372
Seven is neither. A Carroll
diagram gives it a box of its own.

27
00:01:55.592 --> 00:01:59.586
There are many ways to show
it. Choose one, and say why.

28
00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:03.894
Not everything can be counted.
Some things only might happen.

29
00:02:04.114 --> 00:02:07.039
The sun will come up tomorrow.
That one will happen.

30
00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:11.532
You will not roll a seven on an
ordinary dice. It has no seven.

31
00:02:11.752 --> 00:02:16.210
A coin might land on heads. Might
is not the same as will not.

32
00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.727
Spin the arrow thirty times
and keep the score as you go.

33
00:02:19.947 --> 00:02:23.988
Red sixteen, blue nine, gold
five. Red has the most room.

34
00:02:24.488 --> 00:02:28.528
Ask a question whose answers
vary, then count with a tally.

35
00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:32.463
Read a pictogram by its key,
and a bar chart by its height.

36
00:02:32.683 --> 00:02:36.584
Sort with hoops or boxes.
Some things only might happen.
